class:: GeometryReverb
summary:: Moving shoebox direct/early paths with a shared FDN late field
categories:: UGens>Reverbs, Libraries>CamaraObscura
related:: Classes/DarkVelvetReverb, Classes/GeometryReverb, Classes/GroupedFDN, Classes/ModalPlate, Classes/ModalReverbBank, Classes/RIRFDN, Classes/VelvetFDN, Guides/CamaraObscura

description::

image::../Guides/ir_geometry.png#GeometryReverb shows distinct high-amplitude early reflections preceding the diffuse late tail. This plot highlights the temporal gap between the direct geometric paths and the subsequent FDN wash.::

Conceptually, a geometry-based reverb attempts to simulate the actual physical paths that sound takes when bouncing around a room, rather than just statistically approximating the decay. By calculating the exact timing, direction, and attenuation of the direct sound and early reflections bouncing off the walls, floor, and ceiling, it creates a much more convincing spatial impression of being in a real physical space.

Technically, `GeometryReverb` utilizes the image-source method for a shoebox-shaped room to generate up to six first-order early reflections. It interpolates these paths in real-time as the source position moves, preventing zipper noise. To avoid the extreme computational cost of modeling thousands of higher-order reflections, the early reflection engine seamlessly hands off the remaining energy to a shared Feedback Delay Network (FDN) late-field core, offering an efficient hybrid approach that marries spatial accuracy with computational efficiency.

GeometryReverb interpolates a prepared six-dimensional source/listener
shoebox grid once per server block and linearly ramps every path parameter
across that block. A shared fractional input delay renders one direct path and
six first-order image-source reflections; individual one-pole filters and
constant-power horizontal panning use the prepared material/direction data.
A shared FDN begins after its shortest delay to form the late field.

Output is stereo wet audio. Early paths are normalized by the square root of
the fixed path count for headroom while each stored gain remains
inverse-distance. Positions are clipped to the room. The first rotation value
is yaw in radians; pitch and roll are accepted for API stability but ignored.
Source yaw applies a simple front-facing directivity and listener yaw rotates
horizontal panning. There is no elevation, HRTF, diffraction, occlusion,
mesh, or higher-order reflection model.

All geometry is prepared outside the callback. Grid interpolation costs 64
corner combinations per block and per-path rendering is linear in seven
paths. Reset clears early delay/filter and late FDN state. The public API has
no freeze.

The closest vanilla construction is link::Classes/DelayC:: plus a reverb.
GeometryReverb differs by connecting delay, attenuation, filtering, and
direction to a validated moving geometric scene, while deliberately limiting
the first release to a shoebox.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: input
Mono audio input.

argument:: sceneBuf
Loaded link::Classes/GeometryReverbScene::.

argument:: sourcePosition
Three room coordinates in metres.

argument:: listenerPosition
Three room coordinates in metres.

argument:: sourceRotation
Three angles. Only yaw, the first value, is currently rendered.

argument:: listenerRotation
Three angles. Only yaw, the first value, is currently rendered.

argument:: earlyLevel
Runtime level of direct and first-order paths.

argument:: lateLevel
Runtime level of the shared FDN.

argument:: reset
Rising edge clears all early and late state.

returns::
Stereo wet audio Array.

method:: kr
Unavailable; audio-rate only.

examples::

The following examples have been structured into individual blocks so you can easily run them step-by-step.

code::
// Boot the server first!
s.boot;

// 1. Prepare the model and source material
(
~path = "~/Desktop/SuperCollider UGen Ports/04-birosca/CamaraObscura/sounds/".standardizePath;

~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "vocal.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "drum1.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "drum2.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "guitar.wav", channels: [0]);
// ~source = Buffer.readChannel(s, ~path +/+ "violin.wav", channels: [0]);

~model = GeometryReverbScene.shoebox([5, 5, 5], 0.3, sampleRate: s.sampleRate, seed: 1, grid: 2, lateT60: 1).load(s);
)

// 2. Play the reverb (wait a moment for the buffers to load before executing this)
(
x = {
    var input = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~source, BufRateScale.kr(~source), loop: 1);
    // var input = SoundIn.ar(0); // alternative live input
    
    var fade = EnvGen.kr(Env([0, 1], [0.12]));
    var wet = GeometryReverb.ar(input, ~model, [2, 2, 2], [5, 5, 5], [1, 1, 1])[0];
    
    (input + wet) * fade * 0.25 ! 2;
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
x.free;
~source.free;
~model.free;
::

// Creative Example: Moving sound source inside the geometry
code::
// 1. Load the model
~creativeModel = ~creativeModel = GeometryReverbScene.shoebox([10, 8, 4], 0.1, sampleRate: s.sampleRate, grid: 2, lateT60: 2).load(s);

// 2. Play
(
y = {
    var sourceX = SinOsc.kr(0.1).range(1, 9);
    var sourceY = SinOsc.kr(0.15).range(1, 7);
    GeometryReverb.ar(Dust.ar(5), ~creativeModel, [sourceX, sourceY, 2], [10, 8, 4], [2, 2, 2])[0]
}.play;
)

// 3. Clean up
y.free;
~creativeModel.free;
::
